extends /views/templates/layouts/_base

append variables
	- pagetitle = "Chart types | Donut"
	- pagedescription = "Donut charts in Tablechart: parts of a whole as a ring, with an optional total in the middle."

block content
	.lane
		.container
			.page-header
				h1 Donut
				p.lead Parts of a whole, as a ring.

	+section("Donut chart")
		p Each row is a slice. The slices start at the top and go clockwise, in the order of the table. The names are shown in a legend under the ring.

		+chart
			figure(data-chart="donut")
				figcaption
					b Where the crop went
					br
					span thousands of apples
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Juiced', '38.2'], ['Eaten fresh', '18.1'], ['Sold whole', '12.4'], ['Composted', '5.8']]
							tr(data-kind=row[2])
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

	+section("The number in the middle")
		p If a row has #[code data-kind="total"], its value is shown in the middle, and it is not a slice. If there is no total row, the middle shows the sum of the slices.
		p Screen readers read the table, not the middle of the ring. So if there is no total row, Tablechart adds a hidden row to the bottom of the table with the sum, named "Total". If you want to use another name, set the #[code totallabel] option. With #[code data-chart-keys], the name gets a translation key that ends in #[code -total].

		+chart(null, '4')
			figure(data-chart="donut")
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Picked', '74.5', 'total'], ['Juiced', '38.2'], ['Eaten fresh', '18.1'], ['Sold whole', '12.4'], ['Composted', '5.8']]
							tr(data-kind=row[2])
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

	+section("A sweep, step by step")
		p With #[code data-chart-animate="sweep"] and steps, the slices of one step start together, at the same speed. Slices of one step that are next to each other sweep as one. Here the first and third slice are there from the start, and the other two arrive together on the next step, with the same #[code data-fragment-index]. See #[a(href=url('/features/steps.html')) Step by step] and #[a(href=url('/styling/animation.html#grow-or-sweep')) Grow or sweep]. This example is slower, so you can follow it.

		+style.
			.slow-sweep {
				--tablechart-animation-element-duration: 1.5s;
			}

		.card
			.demo
				figure.slow-sweep(data-chart="donut" data-chart-animate="sweep")
					table
						tbody
							each row in [['Elstar', '42', false], ['Jonagold', '28', true], ['Braeburn', '18', false], ['Gala', '12', true]]
								tr(data-fragment=row[2] ? '' : false data-fragment-index=row[2] ? '1' : false)
									th= row[0]
									td= row[1]
			+codeblock('HTML', null, '1,5,7').
				<figure class="slow-sweep" data-chart="donut" data-chart-animate="sweep">
					<table>
						<tbody>
							<tr><th>Elstar</th><td>42</td></tr>
							<tr data-fragment data-fragment-index="1"><th>Jonagold</th><td>28</td></tr>
							<tr><th>Braeburn</th><td>18</td></tr>
							<tr data-fragment data-fragment-index="1"><th>Gala</th><td>12</td></tr>
						</tbody>
					</table>
				</figure>

	+section("Next")
		p By default, all slices use one colour, each one a bit lighter. If you want to set a colour for each slice, see #[a(href=url('/styling/colours.html')) Colours].
